/**
 * GENERATED FROM BLENDER - do not hand-edit.
 *
 * The wood chain's first seven tiers. They were box primitives assembled in
 * TypeScript, and the problem with that was not that the geometry was wrong -
 * it was that a bevel-less box filled with three flat values is EXACTLY the
 * hand-drawn isometric polygon it replaced. Nothing about having real
 * geometry was visible, so the pipeline bought nothing.
 *
 * THE BEVEL IS THE WHOLE POINT. A rounded arris catches a bright line where
 * it faces the key and a dark one where it turns away, and that pair of lines
 * along every edge is the cue that says "solid" rather than "three shapes in
 * three tones". It also has to be WIDE - the first pass used 0.012, about
 * three pixels, which is present in the data and invisible on screen.
 *
 * Bevel 0.034 over three segments, clamped so it cannot eat a thin plank,
 * then Shade Auto Smooth at 30 degrees - the bevels round off, the flats stay
 * flat.
 *
 * Coordinates are RAW, in the same unit space the TypeScript versions used
 * (roughly one unit to a board cell), not normalised - the tiers have to stay
 * the right size relative to each other, and `renderMesh({ center: true })`
 * handles placement.
 */

